岡市":"Anciently Kozukata; first Morigaoka, then 森岡, \"wooded hill\", until the Nanbu lords wrote it 盛岡, \"a hill that rises and prospers\". Another view derives it simply from the wooded hill.","宮古市":"Several theories; most often traced to miyako, \"capital\", as the economic and cultural centre of the Hei district. Others read it as \"place of a shrine\" or \"deep-mountain dwelling\".","大船渡市":"\"The great landing place for boats.\"","花巻市":"Called Toriyagasaki in the Middle Ages. Theories include a famed horse pasture, \"the flower ranch\"; spring petals floating on the Kitakami River; and the Ainu pana, \"land opening downstream\".","北上市":"Thought to derive from the ancient name Hitakami. The present city was named in 1954, when Kurosawajiri and six other towns and villages merged.","久慈市":"Found as a district name from the 14th century. Theories: a \"pinched\" landform, a coast eaten away by the sea, or kutsu, a landing at the river mouth.","遠野市":"Either the plain of \"far Hei\", beyond the Hei district, or simply a distant plain among the mountains; an Ainu origin, to-nup (\"lake-plain\"), is also proposed.","一関市":"From ichi-no-seki, the first weir built against Kitakami River floods; another view links it to the barriers of Hiraizumi, as the city has an Ichinoseki, Ninoseki and Sannoseki.","陸前高田市":"Rikuzen is the old province name; Takata is said to come from the sacred rice fields of Mount Hikami. In the 1955 merger the name of the central town, Takata, was given the prefix Rikuzen.","釜石市":"Theories include stone for making iron pots (kama), Ainu words for \"where fish are dried\" or \"where there is a flat rock\", and pot-shaped stones.","二戸市":"From the Heian-era \"nine gates\" (kunohe) system of Nukanobu district, where the gates of the stockade were numbered one to nine, each with a horse pasture. The city took the name in 1972."}},"kyu":{"03201":{"t":59,"k":34773,"r":[["Morioka Domain",34773]]},"03202":{"t":47,"k":8733,"r":[["Morioka Domain",6951],["Sendai Do